---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const stories = (await getCollection('stories')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Base
  description="Christopher James Kites: coming back to hands-on development, building with AI coding agents, and keeping a record of what actually works."
  track="plain"
  counterpart="/technical/"
>
  <h1>Christopher James Kites</h1>

  <section id="about">
    <p class="lede">
      I'm Christopher James Kites, an engineer coming back to hands-on development after about seven years away
      from regular coding. This time I'm building with AI coding agents as collaborators. I'm less interested in
      what agents promise than in what actually happens when you work with one, so I keep a record of every
      session: what I set out to do, what got done, what went wrong, and what went right. This site is where I
      share what holds up.
    </p>
    <p>
      My background is in information security. I studied Informatics at the University of Washington, focused on
      information assurance and cybersecurity, and it shapes how I work with agents. The questions I keep coming back
      to are what an agent can reach, what it actually changed, and how I'd know.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what">
    <h2>What I'm Doing</h2>
    <p>
      Every time I sit down with an AI agent to build something, the session gets written up. Not just the
      wins: the ordinary sessions too, the ones where nothing much happens. They're how I can see what's routine
      from what's rare, and you can't tell what works if you only keep the memorable ones. Over time, the
      write-ups show patterns: where the agent can be trusted, where its work needs checking, and where I was the
      one who needed checking.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a pattern holds up, it becomes a habit, or a test that runs every time and fails if the problem comes
      back. When it doesn't hold up, that gets written down too.
    </p>
    <p>
      The notebook itself stays private. What I share here are the findings that are still true and useful with
      every name, machine, and number taken out, so you'll get the lessons without the specifics.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are two reasons for starting that strict. An agent drafts most of the writing here, and a strict rule
      leaves less room for a slip to put something private on the page. And the names of machines, accounts, and
      setups are among the first things someone looking for a way in would collect. Leaving them out costs a reader very little. With
      closer review, I may share more over time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="not">
    <h2>What This Isn't</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It isn't a ranking of AI models. I note which model did what, but I'm not benchmarking them.</li>
      <li>
        It isn't advice for everyone. It's one person's work, so what I find is true for me until someone else
        checks it.
      </li>
      <li>
        It isn't proof that anything is secure. I record the safeguards I add and what happens when they're tested,
        but a safeguard I've written down isn't one I've proven.
      </li>
      <li>It isn't automation for its own sake. Nothing gets automated until the notebook shows it's worth it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="stories">
    <h2>Stories</h2>
    <ul>
      {stories.map((story) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/stories/${story.id}/`}>{story.data.title}</a>: {story.data.description}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="journey">
    <h2>The Journey So Far</h2>
    <p>
      From an empty notebook in September 2026 to the habits and safeguards I work with now.{' '}
      <a href="/journey/">See the journey →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="panel">
    Comfortable with AI workflows and want the details? Every page has a twin, or a section, on the technical
    side. <a href="/technical/" data-dig="down">Dig deeper ↓</a>
  </p>
</Base>
